course: dictionary
lesson: 5052
title: Asking for information
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To inquire means to ask for information about something. You might inquire about the times of trains at a station. Inquire is a more formal word than ask, so you will often see it on signs, in letters and in the news.
explain: The word can also mean asking someone politely. «May I inquire your name?» means "May I ask your name, please?" It sounds polite and a little formal, so you would hear it from a teacher or a shop worker more often than from a friend in the playground.
explain: Inquire can also mean to investigate officially, which means looking carefully into something to find out the truth. Officials inquire into accidents and complaints. Look at the words around it: an accident is something that needs checking, so inquire into means look into it properly.
explain: In the UK you may see enquire instead of inquire. Both spellings mean the same thing. «So good of you to inquire!» means someone was kind enough to ask how you are.
fact: Inquire means to ask for information.
fact: «Inquire» is a more formal word than «ask».
fact: Inquire about means to ask about something, as in inquire about the times of trains.
fact: Inquire into means to investigate officially, as in inquire into an accident.
fact: Inquire after someone means to ask how they are.
fact: Enquire is the UK spelling of inquire, and both mean the same thing.
exercise: You want to know when the next train leaves, so you go to the desk to inquire about the times. What does inquire mean here? | To run for the train | To shout loudly across the platform | *To ask for information | To buy a ticket and leave
exercise: In «May I inquire your name?», which word means about the same as inquire? | Sing | Shout | *Ask | Hide
exercise: Officials inquire into an accident. What does inquire into mean? | Pay for the damage | *Investigate officially | Forget about it | Celebrate with a party
test: Which phrase means to ask how someone is? | inquire into an accident | inquire within | *inquire after someone | inquire about the times
test: Which spelling is the UK spelling of inquire? | endure | *enquire | injure | inquest
exam: In «So good of you to inquire!», what is the person most likely doing? | Buying a train ticket | Shouting across a busy road | Hiding a secret | *Asking how you are
